The '''2003 UEFA Cup final''' was played on 21 May 2003 between [[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] of Scotland and [[FC Porto|Porto]] of Portugal. Porto won the match 3–2 in [[Overtime (sports)#Association football|extra time]] thanks to a goal from [[Derlei]]. 

Before this game, no club from Scotland or Portugal had won the [[UEFA Europa League|UEFA Cup]].

The game had what UEFA described as "the largest travelling support to have assembled for a single game"<ref>{{cite news |title=A city divided: half in Glasgow, half in Porto for cup final |author=Paul Kelbie |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/european/a-city-divided-half-in-glasgow-half-in-seville-for-cup-final-590914.html |work=The Independent |archive-url=https://archive.today/20090514225350/http://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/european/a-city-divided-half-in-glasgow-half-in-seville-for-cup-final-590914.html |archive-date=14 May 2009 |url-status=dead |access-date=6 March 2009 |location=London |date=22 May 2003}}</ref> – around 80,000 Celtic fans travelled to [[Seville]] for the final.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/observer/pictures/0,8561,972767,00.html |publisher=[[The Guardian]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070313084149/http://observer.guardian.co.uk/gallery/0,8561,972767,00.html |archive-date=13 March 2007 |access-date=17 June 2009 |url-status=live |title=Celtic in Seville: Observer Sport Monthly index}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Battle of Piccadilly |url=http://www.manchestereveningnews.co.uk/news/greater-manchester-news/battle-of-piccadilly-953792 |work=[[Manchester Evening News]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140219065414/http://www.manchestereveningnews.co.uk/news/greater-manchester-news/battle-of-piccadilly-953792 |archive-date=19 February 2014}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/report?id=98023&cc=5739 |title=Report: Celtic vs FC Porto – UEFA Cup |access-date=16 June 2007 |archive-date=25 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110525132604/http://soccernet.espn.go.com/report?id=98023&cc=5739 |url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Celebrating Celtic pride in the heart of Andalusia |url=https://www.fifa.com/newscentre/news/newsid=90249.html |publisher=[[FIFA]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131023065227/http://www.fifa.com/newscentre/news/newsid=90249.html |archive-date=23 October 2013}}</ref> For this turnout and the manner in which they conducted themselves, Celtic fans received an award from FIFA and UEFA, winning the [[FIFA Fair Play Award]] that year and being presented with a formal recognition from [[UEFA]] at a home match the following season.<ref>{{cite web |title=Fan Award |work=[[FIFA]] |url=https://www.fifa.com/en/mens/awards/gala/0,2418,73590,00.html?articleid=73590 |access-date=12 June 2007 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070411132458/http://www.fifa.com/en/mens/awards/gala/0%2C2418%2C73590%2C00.html?articleid=73590 |archive-date=11 April 2007 |url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.thecelticwiki.com/page/2003-05-21%3A+Celtic+2-3+Porto,+UEFA+Cup+-+Match+Reports |title=Celtic 2–3 Porto, UEFA Cup – Match Reports |work=thecelticwiki|access-date= 21 May 2015|archive-date= 26 June 2015|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20150626113336/http://www.thecelticwiki.com/page/2003-05-21%3A+Celtic+2-3+Porto,+UEFA+Cup+-+Match+Reports|url-status= live}}</ref>

